

From: Kyrylo Tkachov <ktkachov@nvidia.com>

A bytecode interpreter written with a switch at its loop head normally has one
jump-table dispatch.  Every opcode transition returns to the same indirect
branch instruction.  This can make indirect target prediction less effective.
The existing compgotos pass already gives each eligible predecessor its own
computed goto.  Apply the same transformation to the jump-table form.

The guarded tablejump is a short chain rather than one block:

  handler -> fetch and range check -> tablejump

Collect the single-predecessor fall-through chain and copy it into eligible
predecessors.  Take a block into the chain only through a plain fall-through
edge: a jump at its end that also targets the next block would still name
the original block after the edge moves to the copy.  The copies share the
table through the representation added by the cfgrtl patch.  Require a
direct DFS back edge into the collected chain head before the first
tablejump duplication.  This limits the new transformation to a loop head.
Recursive copies do not repeat the test.  Computed gotos keep their existing
behavior.

Use the existing max-goto-duplication-insns budget.  Charge the tablejump as
one budget unit and every other instruction at its size, as for computed
gotos.  A zero budget rejects the transformation.  Require the target's
can_share_jump_table_p hook to permit sharing and reject partitioned
functions.

Reject functions that use stack registers.  Stack-register conversion can
split outgoing edges to reconcile different stack layouts, but a shared-table
edge cannot be changed for only one dispatch.

Set PROP_rtl_allow_tablejump_duplication only while the pass scans and copies
dispatches.  Clear it before CFG cleanup and before any later pass runs.

switch-dispatch-run-5.c sends every dispatch copy to every case and to
out-of-range values, and checks the exact path with a trace.  run-6.c and
run-7.c give handlers enough code that some copies are far from the table,
with and without PIC.  run-8.c has an asm goto that targets its own
fall-through block above the switch.  switch-dispatch-hwloop.c and the bfin
and xtensa tests reach a hardware loop through a shared table.  In
switch-dispatch-placement.c the original dispatch is deleted after a noreturn
call or a return, so its table has to move.  switch-dispatch-4.c checks that
both tables of the trace test are shared on the targets that the new
shared_jump_tables effective target lists.  Plugin tests check per-function
target options, the default hook result, existing instruction-copy vetoes,
and output after eligibility becomes false.

On an AArch64 Vera system, explicit enablement improves 714.cpython_r by
10.3%, mawk by 6.9%, and QuickJS-ng by 24.9%.

Jeff approved v3 of this patch (3/7), provided that the patches it
depends on are approved:
https://inbox.sourceware.org/gcc-patches/d1506ad7-1796-4ae9-8c83-69e9b23ee8a0@oss.qualcomm.com/

